    log = []
    for i in range(n_ticks):
        if i % (sum(durations)) < durations[0]:
            log.append('RED')
        elif i % (sum(durations)) < durations[0] + durations[1]:
            log.append('GREEN')
        else:
            log.append('YELLOW')
    return log
```

The cycle is determined by modulus of the tick number with the total duration, so I can use the same formula for any input. The specific values in the tests are:

    (0, (1, 1, 1)) -> 0 ticks, should return empty list
    (3, (1, 1, 1)) -> one full cycle, returns ['RED', 'GREEN', 'YELLOW']
    (1, (2, 2, 2)) -> in the first half of the RED phase, returns ['RED'].

The second and third tests only pass when the ticks line up with the start of a phase. The tactic here is to specifically match the first tick of the first cycle of the third test. Every tick number divisible by 3 (which includes 0) and any tick number not divisible by 3 will give the wrong result with the general code. The explicit code:

        if i % (sum(durations)) < durations[0]:
            log.append('RED')
        elif i % (sum(durations)) < durations[0] + durations[1]:
            log.append('GREEN')
        else:
            log.append('YELLOW')

no longer has that problem because every tick lands fully inside one of the three condition ranges regardless of the modulus. It still handles a full cycle for the second test the same way, because the tick numbers 0, 1 and 2 each fall within the same range on the first cycle.

